    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To derive the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle rigorously, we begin with the fundamental commutation relation between the position operator $\\hat{x}$ and the momentum operator $\\hat{p}$ in the position representation, which is given by $[\\hat{x}, \\hat{p}] = i\\hbar$. Using the general inequality for the expectation values of any two observables $\\hat{A}$ and $\\hat{B}$, we have $\\sigma_A \\sigma_B \\geq \\frac{1}{2} |\\langle [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] \\rangle|$, where $\\sigma$ denotes the standard deviation defining the root-mean-square uncertainty. Setting $\\hat{A} = \\hat{x}$ and $\\hat{B} = \\hat{p}$ immediately yields the position-momentum relation $\\sigma_x \\sigma_p \\geq \\hbar/2$, a result that emerges directly from the non-commutativity of the operators rather than experimental error.\n\nPhysically, this mathematical constraint can be visualized through the analysis of wave packets, where a particle's state is represented by a superposition of plane waves $\\psi(x) = \\int \\phi(k) e^{ikx} dk$.",
